# Server
# Railway injects PORT at runtime; locally we default to 3100.
PORT=3100

# Postgres — required.
# All graph state (intent + actual) plus users/projects/billing live in Postgres.
# Graphs are JSONB columns on the projects table.
# Host port is 5544 — see docker-compose.yml.
# In production, Railway's Postgres add-on provides this directly — reference
# the variable in the service's variables panel as ${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}}.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5544/projectx

# Better-auth — required.
# - BETTER_AUTH_API_KEY: the key issued by the better-auth hosted dashboard, read
#   by the dash() infra plugin. Sign in at https://better-auth.com → create a
#   project → copy the key. Server refuses to boot without it.
# - BETTER_AUTH_SECRET: the session-signing secret (32+ chars). MUST stay stable
#   across restarts/redeploys — better-auth HMACs the session cookie with it, so
#   a value that changes between boots logs out every signed-in user. Keep it
#   SEPARATE from BETTER_AUTH_API_KEY so a dashboard-key rotation can't drop live
#   sessions. Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
#   Migration note: when first introducing this on an existing deployment, set it
#   to the CURRENT BETTER_AUTH_API_KEY value so already-issued cookies stay valid
#   (no forced logout); any random 32+ char value works for a fresh deployment.
# - BETTER_AUTH_URL: the public origin of the Fastify server. Used to validate
#   request Origin headers and build absolute URLs in emails.
# - APP_URL: the public origin of the web app. Listed as a trusted origin so
#   the Vite dev server (5173) can hit /api/auth/*.
#
# In single-service production (Fastify serves the Vite build), BETTER_AUTH_URL
# and APP_URL are the SAME — your Railway domain (e.g. https://projectx.up.railway.app
# or a custom domain). Set both to that value.
BETTER_AUTH_API_KEY=
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3100
APP_URL=http://localhost:5173

# Anthropic — optional.
# Powers the Analyze action (per-file classification + description via Claude).
# The server boots without it, but clicking "Analyze" on the dashboard will
# return a 4xx until it's set. Get a key from https://console.anthropic.com/.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Trigger.dev — optional.
# Workers (background jobs) deploy to Trigger.dev cloud. The Fastify server
# boots fine without these; they only matter when a code path dispatches a
# task via task.trigger().
# - TRIGGER_SECRET_KEY: per-environment API key from
#   https://cloud.trigger.dev → project → API keys. Format: tr_dev_*** (dev)
#   / tr_prod_*** (prod). The @trigger.dev/sdk reads this directly from
#   process.env when dispatching.
# - TRIGGER_PROJECT_REF: the project ref from the dashboard (proj_***).
#   Mirrors the value hardcoded in trigger.config.ts.
TRIGGER_SECRET_KEY=
TRIGGER_PROJECT_REF=

# GitHub OAuth app — optional.
# Powers Settings → Integrations → Connect GitHub and the import-from-GitHub
# project flow. Without these the integration UI shows a "not configured"
# message; the rest of the app boots and runs normally.
#
# ⚠️ MUST be an OAuth App, NOT a GitHub App. Same dashboard, different
# products — GitHub Apps ignore OAuth scopes (use permissions instead) and
# won't grant `repo`, so the import flow silently lists no private repos.
# Tell them apart by the Client ID: OAuth Apps = short hex (e.g.
# `c3d9b4e2f1a7b8c9d0e1`); GitHub Apps = starts with `Iv23li...`.
#
# Create the OAuth App:
#   1. https://github.com/settings/developers
#   2. Click the "OAuth Apps" tab (NOT "GitHub Apps")
#   3. "New OAuth App"
#        Application name:     Project X (dev)
#        Homepage URL:         http://localhost:5173
#        Authorization callback URL:
#          http://localhost:3100/api/auth/callback/github
#   4. Generate a Client Secret. Copy the Client ID + Secret below.
#
# The callback URL must match `${BETTER_AUTH_URL}/api/auth/callback/github`.
# Scope requested at link time: `repo` (read + clone, includes private).
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Stripe (post-MVP)
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
